  "evidence": [
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-bark-water-deformation","source_ref":"source:static-practical-birch-bark-water","anchor":{"section":"Interaction of birch bark with water"},"note":"Study reports that water adsorption can cause swelling, shrinkage, bending or rolling of birch bark through unequal layer response. It does not set a soaking duration, temperature, thickness, successful bend or condition of a current sheet.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-bark-form-drying","source_ref":"source:static-practical-birch-bark-water","anchor":{"section":"Interaction of birch bark with water"},"note":"Authors describe holding bark in desired shape during humidification and drying to avoid deformation. This is conservation handling, not a historical recipe or guarantee against delamination.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-yarn-cohesion","source_ref":"source:static-practical-yarn-twist","anchor":{"section":"5. Breaking Strength, p. 89"},"note":"NBS describes twist entwining fibres and resisting their mutual slip under longitudinal load. Experiment is Pima cotton yarn, so transfer to other staple-fibre yarns is an inferred qualitative mechanism.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-yarn-over-twist","source_ref":"source:static-practical-yarn-twist","anchor":{"section":"5. Breaking Strength, pp. 89–90"},"note":"NBS reports strength falls beyond sufficient twist and excessive twist can kink yarn. This is a cotton-study result, not an optimal twist or a direct flax value.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-belt-slip","source_ref":"source:static-practical-belt-friction","anchor":{"section":"Belt Friction; Friction in Flat Belts"},"note":"The teaching reference explains that friction resists relative belt/pulley sliding up to a limit. It does not identify a current belt, material, coefficient, force or successful transmission.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-belt-limit","source_ref":"source:static-practical-belt-friction","anchor":{"section":"Friction in Flat Belts"},"note":"The stated flat-belt limit depends on lower-side tension, static friction coefficient and contact angle. It is a qualitative dependency here, not a setting for a historical drive.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-ash-leaching","source_ref":"source:static-practical-wood-ash-lye","anchor":{"section":"Making Lye"},"note":"NPS describes water passing through wood ash and collecting lye-infused runoff, and calls lye caustic. It gives no concentration or cloth-cleaning outcome.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-practical-wool-alkali-risk","source_ref":"source:static-practical-wool-alkali","anchor":{"section":"Detergent choices"},"note":"RSC says alkaline conditions suit cellulosic fibres but wool and silk are susceptible to degradation. It does not give a safe alkali level, duration, dye response or treatment result.","review_status":"approved"}
